      "content": "# Exploration: Triki Engine — Fase 0\n\n## Current State\n\nGreenfield project. No source files exist yet. The only artifacts are:\n- `logic_triki_SDD_v1.docx` — user spec doc (not readable programmatically; content provided via prompt)\n- `.atl/skill-registry.md` — SDD tooling registry\n- `.claude/settings.local.json` — Claude settings\n\nThis exploration is purely design-space analysis — there is no existing code to read.\n\n---\n\n## 1. API Design Questions — Open Decisions\n\n### 1a. `next_move` contract: RETURN vs CALL\n\nThe doc shows both patterns without disambiguating:\n\n```js\n// Pattern A — callback RETURNS the move\nconst result = runGame(function next_move(state) {\n  if (celda_libre(1,1)) return [1,1]\n  return state.legal_moves[0]\n})\n\n// Pattern B — callback CALLS jugar() internally (implied by jugar() existing)\nconst result = runGame(function next_move(state) {\n  jugar(1,1)\n})\n```\n\n**Implication of Pattern A (return-based):**\n- `runGame` owns move execution — it calls `jugar()` internally after the callback returns\n- Easier to validate: if callback returns nothing or returns an illegal move, `runGame` can throw/report cleanly\n- Easier to test: callback is a pure function, no side effects\n- Enables replay (just replay the return values)\n- Recommended for Fase 0\n\n**Implication of Pattern B (call-based):**\n- Callback has side effects — harder to validate, harder to test\n- `jugar()` must be a global that mutates shared state — creates hidden coupling\n- `runGame` needs to know if `jugar()` was called or not (requires wrapping or checking state diff)\n- Harder to detect \"player didn't make a move\" error\n\n**OPEN QUESTION**: Which is the canonical contract? Pattern A (return) is strictly better for Fase 0 testability. Pattern B only makes sense if the user's educational goal includes teaching side-effect-based APIs. Needs explicit decision.\n\n---\n\n### 1b. Are `celda()` / `celda_libre()` globals or `state` methods?\n\nThe example uses them as free functions without `state.` prefix, implying globals.\n\n**Globals:**\n- Simple to call from the console (`celda(1,1)`)\n- Pollutes `window` in browser context\n- Couples the callback to global mutable state — function is not pure\n- Cannot be tested in isolation without resetting global state\n\n**Methods on `state`:**\n```js\nfunction next_move(state) {\n  if (state.celda_libre(1,1)) return [1,1]\n}\n```\n- Keeps the callback pure — all inputs come through `state`\n- Better for testability and replay\n- Slightly more verbose\n\n**Recommendation**: Make `celda()` and `celda_libre()` both:\n1. Methods on `state` (canonical, testable)\n2. Also exported as top-level convenience functions for console use (that read from internal current-state)\n\nThis satisfies \"paste in console and it works\" without sacrificing testability.\n\n**OPEN QUESTION**: User must decide if educational goal prefers global simplicity or method purity.\n\n---\n\n### 1c. Minimal `state` shape\n\nBased on the API contract and edge cases required:\n\n```js\n{\n  board: [\n    ['X', 'O', ''],\n    ['', 'X', ''],\n    ['', '', 'O']\n  ],                        // 3x3, '', 'X', or 'O'\n  current_player: 'X',     // whose turn it is\n  turn_count: 0,            // 0-8 (max 9 turns)\n  legal_moves: [[0,0], [0,2], ...],  // array of [row, col] pairs\n  last_bot_move: [1,1],     // or null — for bot_jugó_en()\n  winner: null,             // null | 'X' | 'O' | 'draw'\n}\n```\n\n`history` is NOT needed for Fase 0 (no replay/undo feature). Add it in Fase 1 only if needed.\n\n---\n\n### 1d. `bot_jugó_en(row, col)` — last move only or all bot moves?\n\nThe name \"jugó\" (past tense) + \"en\" (at) most naturally means \"did the bot play at this cell (ever)?\", but in a tic-tac-toe context, \"last move\" is the only sensible use — highlighting the bot's most recent move for UI feedback.\n\n**Recommendation**: Returns true only for the bot's MOST RECENT move. Track `last_bot_move` in state.\n\n**OPEN QUESTION**: Confirm with user. If \"all bot moves\", the API is less useful — all bot cells are already readable via `celda()`. The \"last move\" interpretation is more useful for UI highlighting and avoids redundancy.\n\n---\n\n### 1e. Coordinate system: 0-indexed or 1-indexed?\n\nThe example uses `celda_libre(1,1)` for center. Tic-tac-toe has 3x3. If 1-indexed, center = (1,1) = row 1, col 1 in a 1-2-3 system — **this is 0-indexed, center is [1][1] in a 0-2 array**. So `(1,1)` IS the center regardless of whether you call it 0-indexed or 1-indexed, because:\n\n- 0-indexed: rows/cols are 0, 1, 2 → center is (1,1) ✓\n- 1-indexed: rows/cols are 1, 2, 3 → center is (2,2)\n\nThe example `celda_libre(1,1)` for center conclusively means **0-indexed**.\n\n`state.legal_moves[0]` is just array indexing, not coordinates — no contradiction.\n\n**Verdict**: 0-indexed. Center = (1,1). Corners = (0,0), (0,2), (2,0), (2,2). No open question here — this is settled by the example.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Minimax Architecture Options\n\n| Approach | Pros | Cons | Complexity |\n|----------|------|------|------------|\n| **Recursive minimax (plain)** | Simplest to understand, easy to verify, educational | Recomputes same states; for 9 cells this is ~250k nodes max — still fast | Low |\n| **Minimax + memoization** | Avoids recomputation, ~5000 unique states in tic-tac-toe | Slightly more code; cache key design needed | Low-Med |\n| **Pre-computed lookup table** | O(1) lookup, zero runtime computation | Requires offline generation script; less educational; 5000-entry JSON blob | Med |\n| **Alpha-beta pruning** | Cleaner stopping condition, industry-standard | Overkill: tic-tac-toe tree is tiny, pruning saves negligible time | Med |\n\n**Recommendation for Fase 0**: Plain recursive minimax, no memoization.\n\nReasoning:\n1. Tic-tac-toe has at most 9! = 362,880 leaf nodes, but the actual game tree is ~255,168 positions. With early termination on wins, the real search is trivially fast.\n2. Plain minimax is MORE readable and MORE educational — the user's goal is to teach concepts.\n3. Memoization adds complexity (cache key serialization) with zero perceptible performance benefit.\n4. Alpha-beta is correct but adds a parameter that obscures the algorithm's intent.\n5. Lookup table is an optimization that belongs to a later phase if latency ever becomes a concern (it won't).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Module Structure\n\n### Option A — Screaming Architecture (deep folders)\n\n```\nsrc/\n  board/\n    board.js         — state, legal_moves, check_winner\n    board.test.js\n  bot/\n    minimax.js       — pure recursive search\n    bot.js           — entry point: chooses best move\n    bot.test.js\n  engine/\n    engine.js        — game loop, turn management, runGame()\n    validators.js    — edge-case validation (out of range, occupied, etc.)\n    engine.test.js\n  api/\n    api.js           — celda(), celda_libre(), bot_jugó_en(), jugar()\n    api.test.js\n  index.js           — re-exports runGame + API for ESM consumers\n```\n\nPros: Clean separation, Fase 1 (Three.js) imports from `src/index.js` with zero changes.\nCons: 5 folders for 4 modules is slightly heavy for a greenfield solo project.\n\n### Option B — Flat src/\n\n```\nsrc/\n  board.js\n  minimax.js\n  bot.js\n  engine.js\n  api.js\n  validators.js\n  index.js\n```\n\nPros: Simpler, less ceremony for a solo educational project.\nCons: As scope grows, files blend together.\n\n### Recommendation\n\n**Option A** — Screaming Architecture. It maps directly to the domain language (board / bot / engine / api), it's the pattern the user's skill set is built on, and it makes Fase 1 integration explicit: `import { runGame, celda, jugar } from './src/index.js'`.\n\n**Edge-case validators** live in `src/engine/validators.js` — they enforce the contract between the caller and the engine, which is the engine's responsibility, not the board's.\n\n**Fase 1 consumption**: Three.js layer imports from `src/index.js`. The engine exposes a clean ESM interface. No modifications to engine files.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Testing Strategy for Fase 0\n\n| Option | Pros | Cons |\n|--------|------|------|\n| **`node --test`** | Zero deps, no config, ships with Node 18+, works in CI immediately | API slightly less ergonomic than Vitest; no watch mode out of the box |\n| **Vitest** | Better DX, watch mode, `--browser` mode for future, familiar Jest API | Adds `package.json` + `node_modules`, small build step, dependency to maintain |\n\n**Recommendation**: `node --test`.\n\nReasoning:\n1. The stated goal is testable from browser console or Node — zero deps aligns with that philosophy.\n2. Fase 0 has no build step, no bundler. Adding Vitest forces a `package.json` and `node_modules` from day one, contradicting the \"pure JS engine\" constraint.\n3. `node --test` is fully capable for unit tests of pure functions.\n4. If the project grows to need Vitest (e.g., for component tests in Fase 2+), migrate then — don't optimize ahead.\n\n**Minimum test coverage to prove \"engine works\"**:\n- `board.test.js`: `check_winner` detects all 8 win conditions + draw; `legal_moves` returns correct cells for any board state\n- `minimax.test.js`: bot never loses (test all first-move responses); bot wins when player makes a losing move; returns draw when both play optimally from any state\n- `engine.test.js`: `runGame` returns `{ winner: 'O', turns: 5 }` for optimal bot vs. center-first player; all 4 edge cases throw expected errors\n- `api.test.js`: `celda()`, `celda_libre()`, `bot_jugó_en()` return correct values after state transitions\n\n---\n\n## 5. Out of Scope for Fase 0\n\nExplicit exclusions to keep the proposal tight:\n- Three.js — zero visual layer\n- CodeMirror — no in-browser editor\n- Pseudocode transpiler — not until Fase 2+\n- Persistence (localStorage, IndexedDB) — not needed\n- Multiplayer / WebSockets — out of scope\n- Difficulty levels — bot is ALWAYS optimal (minimax full search)\n- Undo / replay — out of scope\n- AI-generated hint system — out of scope\n- `package.json` and npm dependencies — Fase 0 is zero-dep\n\n---\n\n## 6. Browser Console Testability\n\nThree options for \"paste this and it works\":\n\n### Option A — Global `window.runGame` (IIFE bundle)\n\n```html\n\u003c!-- single file, no bundler --\u003e\n\u003cscript src=\"triki-engine.js\"\u003e\u003c/script\u003e\n\u003cscript\u003e\n  const result = window.runGame(function(state) { return state.legal_moves[0] })\n\u003c/script\u003e\n```\nCons: Pollutes global namespace; not reusable as a module.\n\n### Option B — ESM with importmap\n\n```html\n\u003cscript type=\"importmap\"\u003e\n  { \"imports\": { \"triki-engine\": \"./src/index.js\" } }\n\u003c/script\u003e\n\u003cscript type=\"module\"\u003e\n  import { runGame, celda, jugar } from 'triki-engine'\n  const result = runGame(state =\u003e state.legal_moves[0])\n\u003c/script\u003e\n```\nCons: importmap requires explicit HTML file; not directly pasteable into console.\n\n### Option C — Native ESM from console (Chromium devtools)\n\n```js\n// Paste into Chrome DevTools console (if served via local server)\nconst { runGame, celda, jugar } = await import('./src/index.js')\nrunGame(state =\u003e state.legal_moves[0])\n```\nPros: Pure ESM, no bundler, works in modern Chrome/Firefox.\nCons: Requires local server (`python -m http.server` or similar); path must be known.\n\n**Recommendation**: Design the engine as pure ESM (`src/index.js` with named exports). For console testability, document `await import('./src/index.js')` as the canonical \"paste snippet\". A single-file IIFE build can be offered as a Fase 0.5 convenience without changing engine code.\n\n**Fase 1 reuse**: Three.js layer does `import { runGame } from '../engine/src/index.js'` — zero engine modification needed.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Risks and Unknowns\n\n1. **API contract ambiguity (return vs. call)** — HIGH RISK. If this is not decided before the proposal phase, the entire engine architecture forks. Must be settled first.\n2. **Globals vs. state methods** — MEDIUM RISK. Affects testability architecture and the callback signature. Impacts all 4 API functions.\n3. **`bot_jugó_en()` semantics** — LOW-MEDIUM RISK. If it means \"all bot moves\", the function is redundant and should be removed. If \"last move only\", it has a valid use case.\n4. **Coordinate system** — SETTLED. 0-indexed. No risk.\n5. **Browser console delivery** — LOW RISK. ESM dynamic import works in all modern browsers. The only risk is if user expects zero-server-needed (i.e., file:// protocol), which blocks dynamic imports in Chrome.\n6. **Testing tooling** — LOW RISK. `node --test` is stable. Risk only if user requires browser-native test execution (not stated).\n7. **Docx content divergence** — MEDIUM RISK. The `.docx` file was not machine-readable in this session. If it contains additional API contracts or constraints not captured in the prompt, the proposal may need revision.\n\n---\n\n## Recommendation\n\nThe recommendation is to proceed to `sdd-propose` with the following decisions PRE-MADE by the user:\n\n1. `next_move` RETURNS the move (Pattern A) — canonical, testable\n2. API functions are methods on `state` + top-level exports for console use\n3. `bot_jugó_en()` = last bot move only\n4. 0-indexed coordinates (already settled)\n5. Plain recursive minimax, no memoization\n6. `node --test` for testing\n7. Screaming Architecture (`src/board/`, `src/bot/`, `src/engine/`, `src/api/`)\n\n### Ready for Proposal\n\nYES — conditional on the user answering the 3 open questions above (items 1, 2, 3 in \"Risks and Unknowns\").",
