      "title": "sdd/triki-engine/proposal",
      "content": "# Proposal: Triki Engine — Fase 0\n\n**Change**: `triki-engine`\n**Phase covered**: Fase 0 (pure JS engine) — Fases 1–3 (Three.js, CodeMirror, transpiler) are explicitly OUT of scope and will be tracked as separate changes.\n**Source of truth**: `sdd/triki-engine/source-spec` (Logic-Triki SDD v1.0, observation #13).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Intent\n\nBuild the pure JavaScript core of Logic-Triki: a deterministic engine that runs a turn-based tic-tac-toe match where the player is a callback that executes arbitrary code calling a fixed API (`celda`, `jugar`, etc.) exposed as globals. The engine must enforce the API contract, run a perfect minimax bot as opponent, detect and report all 10 edge cases from SDD §7 as structured errors, and return a deterministic `{ winner, turns, history }` result. Success means: (a) every test in `node --test` passes, (b) the engine works unchanged in a browser via `\u003cscript type=\"module\"\u003e`, (c) Fases 1–3 can layer Three.js / CodeMirror / a transpiler on top WITHOUT touching engine internals — only by consuming its public API and events. This is the load-bearing foundation; every later phase reads from it.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Scope\n\n### In scope (Fase 0)\n\n- Board module: 3x3 state, `legal_moves`, `check_winner` (rows, cols, diagonals).\n- Bot module: minimax with depth bonus (+10/0/-10 plus depth tiebreaker for faster wins / slower losses).\n- API module: the 5 read functions, 3 read-only state vars, and `jugar(f, c)` action — installed as globals before each player turn, torn down after.\n- Engine module: game loop (player 0 → bot → player 1 → ... up to 5 player turns), sandbox runner that installs/removes globals around each `next_move()` call, edge-case validators.\n- Error types: structured `{ code, message, fix, line? }` thrown by the engine, consumable later by the UI layer.\n- ESM entry `src/index.js` re-exporting `runGame(playerFn, options?)`.\n- Tests with `node --test` (zero deps): one file per module + integration tests for full game scenarios.\n- Browser-runnable: works as `\u003cscript type=\"module\" src=\"src/index.js\"\u003e` with no bundler.\n\n### Out of scope (deferred to later changes)\n\n- Three.js, animations, winning-line visual (Fase 1).\n- CodeMirror editor, per-line reflexes (green/yellow/red/gray), autocomplete (Fase 2).\n- Pseudocode/Python/C++ transpiler, Pyodide WASM (Fase 3).\n- Vite, dev server, bundler config — Fase 0 ships raw ESM.\n- Scoring system (SDD §10) — engine returns `{ winner, turns }`; weighting is UI concern.\n- Reflex states, speed/delay controls, `setTimeout` — engine runs synchronously.\n- Language selection screen.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Approach\n\nHigh-level architecture: **globals-based sandbox + ESM modules + minimax + node --test**, organized via Screaming Architecture.\n\n### 3.1 Module layout\n\n```\nsrc/\n  board/   — pure functions: makeBoard, applyMove, legalMoves, checkWinner, isFull\n  bot/     — minimax(board, depth, isMaximizing) returning {move, score}\n  api/     — buildPlayerAPI(state) → {globals object}, errorCodes, TrikiError class\n  engine/  — runGame(playerFn, options), runPlayerTurn (install/teardown globals), edge-case validators\n  index.js — re-exports runGame\n```\n\n### 3.2 Key design choices and rationale\n\n- **Globals-based sandbox** (the SDD's canonical resolution): the engine writes the API onto a target object (`globalThis` in Node, `window` in browser) before calling `playerFn()`, then deletes those keys after the turn returns. Player code calls `jugar(f, c)` as a side-effect; the engine tracks the call via a private flag on the API object, not via the return value. Justification: this is what the SDD demands so that Fase 3's transpiled pseudocode looks like \"natural\" sequential code with no return statements. Alternative (return-a-move) was rejected because it would force the transpiler to inject artificial `return` semantics into pseudocode that has none.\n- **ESM modules, no bundler**: Fase 0 must be runnable two ways — `node --test` AND `\u003cscript type=\"module\"\u003e` in a browser tab. Using raw ESM is the only path that satisfies both with zero deps. Vite enters in Fase 1.\n- **Minimax with depth bonus** (not alpha-beta): the search space is trivial (9! = 362,880 worst case, in practice far less). Depth bonus naturally prefers faster wins and delayed losses, which is what gives the bot the \"personality\" SDD §4.2 expects. We will write a **verification test** that asserts the SDD's 8-step priority list emerges from this scoring on the canonical scenarios; if step 6 (opposite corner) or step 8 (any side) does not emerge naturally, we add a tiebreaker — explicitly noted as a risk.\n- **`node --test` (built-in)**: matches `sdd-init/juegos` testing config. Zero install, zero config. Vitest arrives in Fase 1 when there are visual components worth integration-testing.\n- **Structured errors**: each edge case throws `new TrikiError({ code, message, fix, line })`. `code` is the stable contract (Fase 2's tooltip generator switches on it); `message`/`fix` are human-readable Spanish per SDD §7. `line` is filled in later by Fase 2's reflex layer.\n- **Infinite-loop cap**: the SDD mandates 50-iter detection. Fase 0 implements it by wrapping `playerFn` execution in an \"iteration counter\" — but since we cannot intercept `while` loops in raw JS, Fase 0 ships a coarser cap: a wall-clock timeout per turn (e.g. 100ms) AND documents that fine-grained 50-iter detection will land with the Fase 3 transpiler (which CAN inject counters). This is a known limitation called out below.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Acceptance criteria\n\nFase 0 is done when ALL of these are demonstrably true via `node --test`:\n\n1. **Minimax never loses**: in 100 matches vs a random-move player, bot wins or draws 100% of the time. In a match vs a hard-coded \"perfect\" player, result is always a draw.\n2. **API contract**: each of the 5 read functions returns the spec-defined value at every turn of at least 3 hand-crafted game scenarios (mid-game state queries verified).\n3. **`última_jugada_bot()` on turn 0 returns `[-1, -1]`** without throwing, but throws `ERR_UNUSED_TURN0_LASTMOVE` only if the player uses that value as coords for `jugar()`.\n4. **All 10 edge cases throw the documented `code`**: 8 player-code cases (`ERR_NO_JUGAR`, `ERR_JUGAR_TWICE`, `ERR_CELL_OCCUPIED`, `ERR_INFINITE_LOOP`, `ERR_COORD_OUT_OF_RANGE`, `ERR_UNINIT_VAR`, `ERR_TURN_NOT_COVERED`, `ERR_UNUSED_TURN0_LASTMOVE`) + 2 board-state cases (`ERR_BOARD_FULL_TIE` returns tie without throwing; `WARN_UNINTENDED_VICTORY` returns winner with a hint flag).\n5. **`jugar()` twice idempotency**: first call applies, second logs a warning and is ignored — game continues.\n6. **Game loop termination**: every game terminates in ≤ 9 total moves (5 player + 4 bot) with a valid `{ winner: 'X' | 'O' | 'tie', turns, history }`.\n7. **`history` array**: contains one entry per move with `{ turn, player, move: [f, c] }`, ordered chronologically.\n8. **Browser smoke test**: an `index.html` with `\u003cscript type=\"module\"\u003eimport {runGame} from './src/index.js'\u003c/script\u003e` runs a sample game and logs the result to console — verified manually once, documented in README snippet.\n9. **No mutation leaks**: after `runGame()` returns, none of the API globals (`celda`, `jugar`, `turno`, etc.) remain on `globalThis` / `window`.\n10. **Determinism**: given the same `playerFn`, two consecutive `runGame()` calls produce identical `{ winner, turns, history }` (minimax is deterministic with a stable tie-break).\n\n---\n\n## 5. Risks\n\n- **R1 — Minimax priorities don't match SDD §4.2 naturally**: if depth bonus alone doesn't produce \"opposite corner\" preference (step 6) or \"any free side\" last-resort behavior (step 8), we need explicit tiebreakers. Mitigation: write priority-emergence tests early; add tiebreakers in `bot/` as a sorted preference list applied to equal-score moves.\n- **R2 — Infinite-loop detection is coarse in Fase 0**: without AST instrumentation, we can only timeout via wall-clock. A pathological loop with no `await` will block the event loop until timeout. Mitigation: document the limitation in the engine README; Fase 3 transpiler injects per-loop iteration counters for the true 50-iter cap.\n- **R3 — Globals collisions in browser**: setting `celda`, `jugar`, etc. on `window` could collide with user scripts. Mitigation: namespace via `window.Triki = { celda, jugar, ... }` AND ALSO mirror to bare globals for Fase 3 transpiler compatibility — this is a deferred decision (see §6).\n- **R4 — `ERR_UNINIT_VAR` and `ERR_TURN_NOT_COVERED` can't be detected in raw JS**: these are semantic checks that require knowing the player wrote code structured around `turno`. Raw JS allows `undefined`-as-value silently. Mitigation: Fase 0 detects them only as runtime symptoms (e.g. `jugar(undefined, 0)` → `ERR_COORD_OUT_OF_RANGE` with a hint); full detection waits for the transpiler.\n- **R5 — Determinism in JS object iteration**: minimax tie-breaks must use sorted move lists, not `Object.keys` order. Easy to get wrong; covered by acceptance criterion #10.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Deferred decisions (for spec/design phase to resolve)\n\n- **D1 — `package.json` or bare `.mjs`?** Recommendation: ship a minimal `package.json` with only `\"type\": \"module\"` and `\"scripts\": { \"test\": \"node --test\" }`. Zero deps still, but enables `node --test` auto-discovery of `*.test.js` files without renaming everything to `.mjs`. Spec phase should confirm.\n- **D2 — Error code naming**: proposed `ERR_*` prefix with SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE (`ERR_NO_JUGAR`, `ERR_CELL_OCCUPIED`, `ERR_INFINITE_LOOP`, `ERR_COORD_OUT_OF_RANGE`, `ERR_JUGAR_TWICE`, `ERR_UNINIT_VAR`, `ERR_TURN_NOT_COVERED`, `ERR_UNUSED_TURN0_LASTMOVE`, `ERR_BOARD_FULL_TIE`, `WARN_UNINTENDED_VICTORY`). Spec phase locks the final list.\n- **D3 — Global exposure shape**: namespaced (`window.Triki.celda(...)`) vs raw (`window.celda(...)`) vs BOTH. Recommendation: BOTH — raw globals for the pseudocode transpiler's natural feel, namespaced for explicit JS users and easier teardown. Design phase confirms.\n- **D4 — Infinite-loop cap mechanism**: wall-clock (e.g. 100ms) vs no cap in Fase 0 (defer entirely to Fase 3). Recommendation: wall-clock cap with `ERR_INFINITE_LOOP` code reused later. Design phase decides timeout value.\n- **D5 — Turn-0 `última_jugada_bot()` semantics**: returns `[-1, -1]` silently, or returns the value AND throws on next `jugar()` if used as coords. Recommendation: return silently, throw `ERR_COORD_OUT_OF_RANGE` only when the bad coord reaches `jugar()`. Design phase locks.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Estimated effort\n\n**Original `sdd-init` estimate**: ~1 day.\n**Revised honest estimate**: **1.5–2 days** of focused work.\n\nBreakdown:\n- Board + winner detection: 1.5 hours (trivial, well-known).\n- Minimax + depth bonus + priority-emergence verification: 3–4 hours (the verification tests are the slow part).\n- API globals install/teardown + `jugar()` idempotency guard: 2 hours.\n- Edge case validators (the 10 cases) + structured error type: 3–4 hours (each case needs a test scenario).\n- Game loop + history + determinism: 2 hours.\n- Browser smoke test + minimal README snippet: 1 hour.\n- Buffer for risk R1 (adding tiebreakers if priorities don't emerge): 2–3 hours.\n\nThe original 1-day estimate likely assumed minimax with no priority verification and no structured error layer. Once we honor the SDD's 8-step priority list AND the 10-edge-case contract as testable behaviors, 1.5–2 days is the honest number. Spec phase should treat this as the target.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Next phase\n\n- `sdd-spec` — write delta specs for `engine`, `board`, `bot`, `api` modules: lock the public API surface, error code list, and acceptance test scenarios.\n- `sdd-design` (parallel) — architectural notes on the globals sandbox lifecycle, minimax tiebreaker strategy, and Node-vs-browser environment detection.",
