      "content": "# Triki Engine — Fase 0 Technical Design\n\n**Change**: `triki-engine`\n**Phase**: Fase 0 — pure JS engine\n**Artifact store**: engram\n**Reads**: proposal #14, spec #15, source-spec #13, init #10\n**Status**: design locked — ready for `sdd-tasks`\n\nThis document locks the HOW. The spec already locked the WHAT.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Sandbox Lifecycle — The Core Mechanism\n\n### 1.1 D3 RESOLVED — Globals shape: **BOTH raw + namespaced**\n\nThe engine installs each identifier (`celda`, `celda_libre`, `bot_jugó_en`, `yo_jugué_en`, `última_jugada_bot`, `jugar`, `turno`, `mi_símbolo`, `bot_símbolo`) on `globalThis` BOTH as a bare property AND mirrored inside `globalThis.Triki`.\n\n**Why both:**\n- **Raw globals** — non-negotiable for Fase 3. The pseudocode transpiler must produce code that reads as `si celda(0,0) == \"X\" entonces jugar(0,0)`. Wrapping every identifier as `Triki.celda(...)` defeats the pedagogical model. The proposal and SDD §3 explicitly mandate this shape.\n- **Namespaced (`Triki.*`)** — operational convenience: console UX (`Triki.celda(1,1)` is greppable while debugging), single-handle teardown sanity check (`delete globalThis.Triki` is one line; we can assert `globalThis.Triki === undefined` as a teardown smoke test), Fase 1+ can keep using the namespaced form even after Fase 3 introduces transpiled code that prefers raw.\n- **Collision risk** — accepted. Player code runs in a controlled context (their own editor); the engine ALWAYS wins because it sets the globals immediately before each turn. If the player overwrites them, that is THEIR mistake — and the engine catches it on the next turn anyway because it reinstalls every turn.\n\n**Implementation note**: install via `Object.defineProperty` with `{ configurable: true, writable: false }` for the read-only state vars (`turno`, `mi_símbolo`, `bot_símbolo`) — `configurable: true` because the engine must delete them after each turn. Functions are also defined as non-writable to prevent the player from shadowing `jugar` mid-turn.\n\n### 1.2 How `jugar()` is captured — **module-level pendingMove + idempotency flag**\n\nWhen the player calls `jugar(f, c)`, the engine MUST capture the move and apply it AFTER `playerFn()` returns. Three options were considered:\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A — Sentinel exception thrown by `jugar`** | REJECTED — pollutes try/catch logic, makes `ERR_JUGAR_TWICE` (which must NOT terminate the turn) awkward, and conflates control flow with errors. |\n| **B — Callback registered by engine** | REJECTED — adds an extra indirection layer with no benefit; closure approach below is simpler. |\n| **C — Closure-captured `pendingMove` ref in the API factory** ✅ | CHOSEN |\n\n**Mechanism**: `api/buildPlayerAPI(boardState, turnContext)` returns `{ globals, getPendingMove, getErrors }` where `globals` is the object the engine installs and `getPendingMove()` reads from a closure variable that `jugar(f, c)` sets.\n\n```text\n// pseudocode — NOT to be written verbatim, this is design only\nbuildPlayerAPI(boardState, turnContext):\n  let pendingMove = null\n  let turnErrors = []\n  jugar(f, c):\n    validate coords → if invalid push ERR_COORD_OUT_OF_RANGE to turnErrors; return\n    if pendingMove !== null:\n      push ERR_JUGAR_TWICE to turnErrors; return  // idempotent: ignore second call\n    if cell is occupied:\n      push ERR_CELL_OCCUPIED to turnErrors; return\n    pendingMove = [f, c]\n  return { globals: {...}, getPendingMove: () =\u003e pendingMove, getErrors: () =\u003e turnErrors }\n```\n\nThe engine, after `playerFn()` returns: reads `getPendingMove()`. If `null` → push `ERR_NO_JUGAR`. If non-null → apply via `board.applyMove`. Reads `getErrors()` and merges into the global `errors[]`.\n\n**Why this wins**: synchronous, single source of truth, errors-as-data (not as exceptions for non-terminal cases), and the closure makes each turn's API instance ISOLATED by construction — there is no shared mutable module-level state to clean up.\n\n### 1.3 Globals lifecycle — set before, deleted after, ALWAYS\n\n```text\nrunPlayerTurn(boardState, turnIndex, playerFn, options):\n  api = buildPlayerAPI(boardState, { turn: turnIndex })\n  installGlobals(api.globals)        // Object.defineProperty all entries\n  try:\n    runWithTimeout(playerFn, options.maxTurnMs)\n  catch err:\n    if err is timeout → push ERR_INFINITE_LOOP\n    else if err is ReferenceError → push ERR_UNINIT_VAR (with native message)\n    else if err is TrikiError → push it\n    else → re-throw (engine bug, not player bug)\n  finally:\n    teardownGlobals()                // delete every key, delete globalThis.Triki\n  return { pendingMove: api.getPendingMove(), errors: api.getErrors() }\n```\n\nThe `finally` block is the guarantee for AC-10 (no leaks). It runs even on uncaught exceptions, even on timeout. Teardown deletes each key individually (not via reassign-to-undefined — that leaves the property defined).\n\n### 1.4 State isolation between turns\n\nEach turn gets a FRESH `buildPlayerAPI()` call. The closure is per-turn. There is no module-level mutable state in `api/`. Cross-turn contamination is structurally impossible. The engine itself holds the canonical `boardState` and `history`; the API only reads from a snapshot passed in.\n\n### 1.5 Node vs browser — `globalThis` is the answer\n\nUse `globalThis` everywhere. It is the spec-mandated cross-environment global object (Node 12+, all modern browsers). `window` is NEVER referenced in engine source. In a browser, `globalThis === window`, so `\u003cscript type=\"module\"\u003e` consumers still see `window.celda` after install — works for both the test harness and the browser smoke test with no branching.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Module Boundaries \u0026 Dependency Direction\n\n### 2.1 Final layout\n\n```\nsrc/\n  board/\n    state.js          — makeBoard, applyMove, cloneBoard\n    legal.js          — legalMoves, isFull\n    winner.js         — checkWinner (rows, cols, diagonals)\n  bot/\n    minimax.js        — minimax(board, depth, isMaximizing) → score\n    chooseMove.js     — chooseMove(board) → [f, c]; applies tiebreakers\n    priorities.js     — explicit preference table for opposite-corner / side fallback\n  api/\n    errors.js         — TrikiError class, error code constants\n    buildPlayerAPI.js — factory: (boardState, turnContext) → { globals, getPendingMove, getErrors }\n  engine/\n    sandbox.js        — installGlobals, teardownGlobals, runWithTimeout\n    runPlayerTurn.js  — orchestrates one player turn\n    validators.js     — post-turn validators (WARN_UNINTENDED_VICTORY heuristic)\n    runGame.js        — the public entry: full game loop\n  index.js            — re-exports { runGame }\n```\n\n### 2.2 Dependency graph (no cycles)\n\n```\nindex.js → engine/runGame\nengine/runGame → engine/runPlayerTurn, engine/validators, board/*, bot/chooseMove, api/errors\nengine/runPlayerTurn → engine/sandbox, api/buildPlayerAPI, api/errors, board/*\nengine/sandbox → (nothing — leaf)\nengine/validators → board/winner, api/errors\napi/buildPlayerAPI → api/errors, board/legal, board/state\napi/errors → (nothing — leaf)\nbot/chooseMove → bot/minimax, bot/priorities, board/*\nbot/minimax → board/state, board/legal, board/winner\nbot/priorities → (nothing — leaf)\nboard/state → (nothing — leaf)\nboard/legal → board/state\nboard/winner → (nothing — leaf; reads board directly)\n```\n\nDirection is strictly downward. `board/` and `api/errors` are leaves. `bot/` depends only on `board/`. `api/` depends only on `board/` + `errors`. `engine/` depends on everything else. `index.js` depends only on `engine/runGame`. **Zero circular risk.**\n\n### 2.3 Purity\n\n| Module | Pure? | Side effects |\n|---|---|---|\n| `board/*` | ✅ pure | returns new states, never mutates input |\n| `bot/*` | ✅ pure | reads board, returns moves; clones boards in recursion |\n| `api/errors` | ✅ pure | class + constants |\n| `api/buildPlayerAPI` | controlled side effects | mutates its own closure only; returns handles |\n| `engine/sandbox` | ⚠ impure by design | mutates `globalThis` — but ALWAYS reverts in `finally` |\n| `engine/runPlayerTurn` | impure orchestrator | calls sandbox |\n| `engine/validators` | ✅ pure | reads history + final board |\n| `engine/runGame` | impure orchestrator | top-level loop |\n\nThe impure surface is intentionally minimal: only `engine/sandbox` touches `globalThis`. Everything else is referentially transparent and testable in isolation.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Minimax Implementation Strategy\n\n### 3.1 Signatures\n\n```\nminimax(board, depth, isMaximizing) → number\n  // returns a score from the perspective of the bot ('O')\n  // base cases:\n  //   if checkWinner(board) === 'O' → return 10 - depth\n  //   if checkWinner(board) === 'X' → return -10 + depth\n  //   if isFull(board)              → return 0\n  // recursion:\n  //   isMaximizing: for each legal move, play 'O', recurse with !isMax, depth+1; take max\n  //   minimizing:   for each legal move, play 'X', recurse with !isMax, depth+1; take min\n\nchooseMove(board) → [f, c]\n  // returns the bot's chosen cell\n  // 1. enumerate legalMoves in row-major order ([0,0], [0,1], ..., [2,2])\n  // 2. score each via minimax(boardAfterMove, 1, false)\n  // 3. find max score; collect ALL moves matching it\n  // 4. if exactly one max move → return it\n  // 5. if multiple → apply priorities.js tiebreaker (see §3.3)\n```\n\n### 3.2 Determinism (AC-11)\n\nTwo guarantees:\n- **Row-major enumeration** of legal moves at EVERY recursion level (sorted, not Object.keys order)\n- **First-found wins among equals** — `Array.reduce` with `\u003e` (not `\u003e=`) so the FIRST move with the max score is chosen, deterministically\n\n### 3.3 SDD §4.2 priority verification — primary + fallback\n\n**Primary approach**: TEST FIRST. Write 8 fixture-based tests (one per priority), each with a hand-crafted board where the SDD demands a specific move. Run pure minimax. Inspect which priorities emerge naturally and which don't.\n\n**Expected result based on minimax theory**:\n- Priorities 1–4 (win, block, fork, block-fork) emerge NATURALLY — minimax with depth bonus produces them by construction\n- Priority 5 (center on empty board) emerges NATURALLY — center has the highest branching factor for forks\n- Priorities 6–8 (opposite corner, any corner, any side) DO NOT emerge naturally from pure minimax — these are tie-breaking heuristics over equally-scored draws\n\n**Fallback strategy — CHOSEN: Option A (explicit priority layer)**:\n- `bot/priorities.js` exports `preferAmongEquals(board, equalScoreMoves) → [f, c]`\n- The preference order (applied only when multiple moves tie at the max score):\n  1. If player's last move was a corner AND opposite corner is in `equalScoreMoves` → opposite corner\n  2. If any corner is in `equalScoreMoves` → first corner in row-major order\n  3. If any side cell is in `equalScoreMoves` → first side in row-major order\n  4. Otherwise → first move in row-major order\n\n**Why Option A over Option B (adjust scoring)**:\n- Scoring adjustments (corner bonus, side malus) RISK breaking priorities 1–4 in subtle ways — a +0.1 corner bonus can flip a fork-vs-corner decision incorrectly\n- A separate tiebreaker layer keeps minimax MATHEMATICALLY PURE and concentrates the heuristic in one auditable file\n- Easier to debug: if priority 7 misbehaves, you look at `priorities.js` only\n\n**Test order**: write all 8 priority tests FIRST. Implement plain minimax. Run. Then add `priorities.js` to cover the failing cases (expected: 6, 7, 8). Re-run. All green.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Error Handling Architecture\n\n### 4.1 `TrikiError` class shape\n\n```\nclass TrikiError extends Error {\n  constructor({ code, message, fix, turn }) {\n    super(message)\n    this.name = 'TrikiError'\n    this.code = code      // stable string — Fase 2 switches on this\n    this.fix = fix        // ES message, end-user\n    this.turn = turn      // player-turn index when raised\n  }\n}\n```\n\n`line?` from the proposal is OMITTED in Fase 0 — line numbers require AST instrumentation that lands with Fase 3. The field is RESERVED in spirit (TrikiError can carry it later) but not populated.\n\n### 4.2 Error flow — data, not exceptions, for non-fatal cases\n\nThis is the KEY architectural call: **most \"errors\" are pushed into the per-turn `turnErrors[]` array, NOT thrown**. Only structural failures (ReferenceError from undefined vars, timeout) bubble as exceptions.\n\n| Code | Mechanism | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| `ERR_NO_JUGAR` | Pushed by engine after turn (if `pendingMove === null`) | Not an exception — it's a post-condition check |\n| `ERR_JUGAR_TWICE` | Pushed by `jugar()` itself, returns silently | Spec demands CONTINUE; throwing would terminate |\n| `ERR_CELL_OCCUPIED` | Pushed by `jugar()`, returns silently | Same — CONTINUE |\n| `ERR_COORD_OUT_OF_RANGE` | Pushed by `jugar()` AND by the 4 read functions | Read functions return a safe sentinel (`''` / `false` / `[-1,-1]`) AND push the error |\n| `ERR_INFINITE_LOOP` | Caught from timeout mechanism in `runWithTimeout`, pushed by engine | Timeout is an exception by nature |\n| `ERR_UNINIT_VAR` | Caught from native `ReferenceError`, wrapped as TrikiError, pushed by engine | Native errors must be intercepted |\n| `WARN_UNINTENDED_VICTORY` | Pushed by `engine/validators.js` after game ends | Heuristic only — needs post-game analysis |\n| `ERR_BOARD_FULL_TIE` | NOT an error — just the natural end state | Spec is explicit |\n| `ERR_TURN_NOT_COVERED` | Collapsed into `ERR_NO_JUGAR` for Fase 0 | RISK-1 from spec |\n| `ERR_UNUSED_TURN0_LASTMOVE` | Surfaced as `ERR_COORD_OUT_OF_RANGE` with `fix` referencing `última_jugada_bot()` | Spec resolution |\n\n### 4.3 Errors collection lifecycle\n\n- Each turn produces a `turnErrors[]` from `getErrors()` after `playerFn` returns\n- Engine merges `turnErrors` into the global `errors[]` after each turn\n- The global `errors[]` is returned in `runGame`'s result object\n\n### 4.4 \"Pause\" vs \"Continue\" mapping (spec §3 / SDD §7.3)\n\nSDD says some edges PAUSE and some CONTINUE — but Fase 0 has no UI to pause. Translation rules:\n\n- **PAUSE in SDD** → push error to `errors[]` AND mark turn as lost (no move applied) → game CONTINUES to next turn. The \"pause\" semantic is preserved as data; Fase 2 reads `errors[]` and renders the pause visually.\n- **CONTINUE in SDD** → push warning to `errors[]` AND apply the valid move (e.g. first call of `jugar()` when there's a second).\n\nFase 0 NEVER terminates the game on a player error. Only the natural conditions (win, tie, board full) terminate.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Infinite-Loop Detection in Fase 0\n\n**Decision: wall-clock timeout via `performance.now()` polled inside `jugar()` and inside every API read function.**\n\n### 5.1 Mechanism\n\n`runWithTimeout(playerFn, maxTurnMs)`:\n- Capture `startTime = performance.now()` before calling `playerFn`\n- The closure inside `buildPlayerAPI` knows `startTime` and `maxTurnMs`\n- EVERY API function (`celda`, `jugar`, etc.) first checks: `if (performance.now() - startTime \u003e maxTurnMs) throw new TrikiError({ code: 'ERR_INFINITE_LOOP', ... })`\n- This converts \"infinite loops that call any API\" into bounded errors\n\n### 5.2 The hard case: pure infinite loop with NO API calls\n\n`() =\u003e { while(true) {} }` — pure busy loop, never calls `celda` or `jugar`. JS is single-threaded synchronous; the engine CANNOT interrupt this. The event loop is blocked.\n\n**Position taken: documented limitation, accepted for Fase 0.**\n\nRationale:\n- The transpiled-pseudocode path (Fase 3) will inject a per-iteration counter into `mientras` loops — the TRUE 50-iter cap lives there\n- For Fase 0 testing, we put the test runner's per-test timeout (`node --test --test-timeout=2000`) as a backstop — a pure busy loop fails the test rather than freezing CI\n- README must call this out: \"Fase 0 cannot interrupt a busy loop that never calls the API. This limitation is resolved in Fase 3 via the transpiler's iteration counter.\"\n\nThis is HONEST. Pretending we have full loop detection in raw JS would mislead. The polling-on-API approach catches 95% of realistic cases (a loop that's \"doing something\" hits the API eventually).\n\n### 5.3 Why `performance.now()`\n\nAvailable in both Node (15+) and all browsers as `performance.now()`. Sub-millisecond resolution. No `import` needed in modern Node (it's a global). `Date.now()` works too but has lower resolution and risks system-clock drift artifacts in tests.\n\n---\n\n## 6. History Recording\n\n### 6.1 Where\n\n`history[]` lives as a local array inside `runGame`. It's appended:\n- After a successful player move (in `runGame` after `runPlayerTurn` returns a `pendingMove`)\n- After the bot's move (in `runGame` right after `chooseMove` + `applyMove`)\n- Never appended for failed turns (no-jugar, jugar-twice second call, out-of-range)\n\n### 6.2 Entry shape — LOCKED\n\n```\n{\n  turn: number,         // player-turn index 0..4\n  player: 'X' | 'O',    // X = human player, O = bot\n  move: [number, number] // [fila, col], both 0..2\n}\n```\n\nBot entries share the player-turn index of the turn they follow. Example sequence:\n- turn 0: player plays [1,1] → `{ turn: 0, player: 'X', move: [1,1] }`\n- bot responds at [0,0]       → `{ turn: 0, player: 'O', move: [0,0] }`\n- turn 1: player plays [2,2]  → `{ turn: 1, player: 'X', move: [2,2] }`\n- ...\n\n`elapsedMs` from the question is OMITTED for Fase 0 — not in the spec's locked shape, would add measurement noise to deterministic tests. Can be added in a later change without breaking consumers.\n\n### 6.3 Determinism guarantee\n\nBecause the bot is deterministic and the engine never inserts non-determinism (no `Math.random` anywhere in engine code), `history` is fully reproducible for a given `playerFn`. This satisfies AC-11.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Testing Architecture\n\n### 7.1 Layout — **colocated `*.test.js`**\n\nEach source file gets a sibling `*.test.js`:\n\n```\nsrc/board/state.js + state.test.js\nsrc/board/legal.js + legal.test.js\nsrc/board/winner.js + winner.test.js\nsrc/bot/minimax.js + minimax.test.js\nsrc/bot/chooseMove.js + chooseMove.test.js\nsrc/bot/priorities.js + priorities.test.js\nsrc/api/errors.js + errors.test.js\nsrc/api/buildPlayerAPI.js + buildPlayerAPI.test.js\nsrc/engine/sandbox.js + sandbox.test.js\nsrc/engine/runPlayerTurn.js + runPlayerTurn.test.js\nsrc/engine/validators.js + validators.test.js\nsrc/engine/runGame.js + runGame.test.js\nsrc/index.test.js (smoke: import + run a game)\n```\n\n**Why colocated**: instant discoverability (open module, see its tests next to it), aligns with `node --test` auto-discovery glob (`**/*.test.js`), Screaming Architecture stays intact (each folder is self-contained).\n\n### 7.2 Integration tests — inside `src/engine/runGame.test.js`\n\nEnd-to-end scenarios from spec §8 live in `runGame.test.js`. Unit tests for the loop's internal helpers also live there. No separate `tests/` folder.\n\n### 7.3 Fixtures — inline JS objects\n\nBoard states are 9 cells. Inline literals like `[['X','','O'],['','X',''],['','','']]` are clearer than file-based fixtures. Where multiple tests share the same setup, a top-level `const` per-test-file is sufficient. No fixture infrastructure needed.\n\n### 7.4 `node --test` invocation\n\n`package.json` script: `\"test\": \"node --test\"`. Node auto-discovers `**/*.test.js`. Add `--test-timeout=2000` as a safety net for the busy-loop limitation noted in §5.2.\n\n---\n\n## 8. File Structure — Exhaustive List for `sdd-tasks`\n\n**Project root**:\n```\npackage.json                              ← { \"type\": \"module\", \"scripts\": { \"test\": \"node --test\" } }\nREADME.md                                 ← usage snippets (node + browser), limitations\nindex.html                                ← browser smoke test page\nsrc/\n  index.js                                ← re-exports { runGame }\n  index.test.js                           ← smoke: imports runGame, runs a sample game\n  board/\n    state.js                              ← makeBoard, applyMove, cloneBoard\n    state.test.js\n    legal.js                              ← legalMoves, isFull\n    legal.test.js\n    winner.js                             ← checkWinner\n    winner.test.js\n  bot/\n    minimax.js                            ← minimax(board, depth, isMaximizing) → score\n    minimax.test.js\n    chooseMove.js                         ← chooseMove(board) → [f,c]\n    chooseMove.test.js\n    priorities.js                         ← preferAmongEquals tiebreaker\n    priorities.test.js\n  api/\n    errors.js                             ← TrikiError class + ERROR_CODES constants\n    errors.test.js\n    buildPlayerAPI.js                     ← factory: (boardState, turnCtx) → { globals, getPendingMove, getErrors }\n    buildPlayerAPI.test.js\n  engine/\n    sandbox.js                            ← installGlobals, teardownGlobals, runWithTimeout\n    sandbox.test.js\n    runPlayerTurn.js                      ← one-turn orchestrator\n    runPlayerTurn.test.js\n    validators.js                         ← WARN_UNINTENDED_VICTORY heuristic\n    validators.test.js\n    runGame.js                            ← main loop + integration tests\n    runGame.test.js\n```\n\n**Total source files**: 14 modules + 14 test files + 3 root files = **31 files**.\n\n`sdd-tasks` will turn each into a discrete task (with TDD ordering: tests before implementation where Strict TDD applies).\n\n---\n\n## 9. Browser \"Paste-and-Run\" Delivery\n\n### 9.1 The constraint\n\nBrowser ESM (`\u003cscript type=\"module\"\u003e`) requires HTTP — `file://` blocks `import` due to CORS. The user cannot just double-click `index.html`.\n\n### 9.2 Solution — minimal `index.html` + serve instruction\n\n`index.html` ships at project root:\n\n```html\n\u003c!doctype html\u003e\n\u003cmeta charset=\"utf-8\"\u003e\n\u003ctitle\u003eTriki Engine — Fase 0 smoke test\u003c/title\u003e\n\u003cscript type=\"module\"\u003e\n  import { runGame } from './src/index.js'\n  window.Triki = window.Triki || {}\n  window.Triki.runGame = runGame\n  // Sample player\n  const samplePlayer = () =\u003e { jugar(1, 1) }\n  console.log(runGame(samplePlayer))\n\u003c/script\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eOpen DevTools — see \u003ccode\u003econsole\u003c/code\u003e for the result. Call \u003ccode\u003eTriki.runGame(yourFn)\u003c/code\u003e to run again.\u003c/p\u003e\n```\n\n`README.md` documents: `npx serve .` (or any HTTP server) → open `http://localhost:3000`. We do NOT add `serve` as a dependency — zero-deps is a hard rule for Fase 0. The user installs it ad-hoc via `npx`.\n\n### 9.3 Node usage (no HTML needed)\n\n```\nimport { runGame } from './src/index.js'\nconst result = runGame(() =\u003e jugar(1, 1))\nconsole.log(result)\n```\n\nWorks directly because `package.json` has `\"type\": \"module\"`.\n\n### 9.4 Why this satisfies the proposal's acceptance criterion #8\n\nThe smoke test is the `index.html` page itself — load it once, see the console log, done. No automation needed in Fase 0.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Open Questions for `sdd-apply`\n\nAll major architectural decisions are LOCKED in this design. The following are micro-decisions explicitly DELEGATED to the apply phase (intentionally — they are implementation details, not architecture):\n\n- **Q1 — `installGlobals` property descriptor for functions**: spec says `turno`/`mi_símbolo`/`bot_símbolo` must be read-only. Functions (`celda`, `jugar`, etc.) MAY also be installed as `writable: false` to prevent player from shadowing them mid-turn. Recommendation: do install as non-writable; if any test fails because of strict-mode assignment errors, downgrade those specific cases. Apply phase decides on first test failure.\n- **Q2 — Spanish character handling in error messages**: `mi_símbolo` / `última_jugada_bot` contain non-ASCII characters. Source files MUST be UTF-8 encoded (default for modern Node). No BOM. Apply phase verifies by reading back its own writes.\n- **Q3 — `chooseMove` when no legal moves exist**: only happens on a full board, which means the game should have already ended. Apply phase should add a defensive throw (`assert legalMoves.length \u003e 0`) — this is engine-bug detection, not player-error handling, so it can be a plain `Error`, not `TrikiError`.\n- **Q4 — Test ordering when Strict TDD is OFF**: `sdd-init` flagged `strict_tdd: false` for Fase 0 (no runner installed yet — but `node --test` IS installed by virtue of Node itself, so this is stale). Apply phase should still follow test-first within each module: write `*.test.js`, watch it fail, implement, watch it pass. This is good practice independent of the strict-TDD flag.\n\n---\n\n## 11. ADR Summary — Locked Decisions\n\n| ADR | Decision | Rejected alternatives | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| ADR-1 | Both raw AND namespaced globals on `globalThis` | raw-only, namespaced-only | Fase 3 transpiler needs raw; ops needs namespaced |\n| ADR-2 | `jugar()` capture via closure `pendingMove` | sentinel exception, callback registration | Synchronous, errors-as-data, isolated per-turn |\n| ADR-3 | Per-turn `buildPlayerAPI()` factory (no module state) | shared module state with reset | Cross-turn isolation by construction |\n| ADR-4 | `globalThis` for env detection (no `window`/`global` branching) | `typeof window !== 'undefined'` checks | spec-mandated cross-env; zero branching |\n| ADR-5 | Minimax priorities 6–8 via separate `priorities.js` tiebreaker | scoring adjustments inside minimax | Keeps minimax mathematically pure; concentrates heuristic |\n| ADR-6 | Errors-as-data in `errors[]` for non-fatal cases | throw everywhere | Spec demands CONTINUE for most errors; data model is cleaner |\n| ADR-7 | `performance.now()` polling inside API functions for timeout | atomics, web workers, AST instrumentation | Only viable sync-only option; busy-loop limitation documented |\n| ADR-8 | Colocated `*.test.js` (not `tests/` folder) | separate test directory | Screaming Architecture; `node --test` auto-discovery |\n| ADR-9 | No `elapsedMs` in `HistoryEntry` for Fase 0 | include it | Not in locked spec shape; adds test noise |\n| ADR-10 | No `serve` as dependency; document `npx serve` in README | bundle serve, ship dev server | Zero-deps rule; ad-hoc is fine for Fase 0 |",
